{
  "schemaVersion": "1.11",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Image uploads: API Gateway, Lambda, SQS and S3",
    "description": "A photo app's upload path on managed AWS services. A mobile app (70%) and a web uploader call API Gateway, whose integration timeout is lowered to 8 s so it gives up before the clients' 10 s. A Node.js Lambda stores the original in S3, writes a metadata item to DynamoDB (on demand) and sends a resize job to SQS, then answers. A Python Lambda behind the queue (reserved concurrency 300, with the event source's maximum concurrency set to the same 300 so the poller never invokes more than the function may run) fetches the original, writes three sizes to a second bucket and marks the item ready. Half the requests are status reads. Each path retries in one place: the mobile app retries an upload (its photo id makes that safe), and SQS redelivers a failed job up to five times, then moves it to a dead-letter queue. The visibility timeout is 6 minutes, six times the function's 60 s timeout, as AWS advises. Traffic is 150 req/s until a post goes viral from 20 to 50 s (900 req/s). The engine has no payload sizes, so an upload's transfer time is not modelled, and the API writes to S3 itself where many apps hand out a presigned URL instead. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "serverless",
      "lambda",
      "sqs",
      "s3",
      "dynamodb",
      "cold-start",
      "backlog",
      "client-groups"
    ]
  },
